{"id":"e40c5336-5d98-4d9e-bc5b-09d5f72392bb","object":"chat.completion","created":1785012522,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Grammar-guided node reduction on a parse tree.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    \\n    # Map id to node dict\\n    node_by_id = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    \\n    # Build parent->children mapping\\n    from collections import defaultdict\\n    children = defaultdict(list)\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        pid = n.get('parent')\\n        if pid is not None:\\n            children[pid].append(n['id'])\\n    \\n    # Find root (parent None)\\n    root_id = None\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        if n.get('parent') is None:\\n            root_id = n['id']\\n            break\\n    if root_id is None:\\n        raise ValueError('No root node found')\\n    \\n    # Helper to get all descendant ids (including self)\\n    def subtree_ids(node_id):\\n        result = [node_id]\\n        stack = [node_id]\\n        while stack:\\n            cur = stack.pop()\\n            for child in children.get(cur, []):\\n                result.append(child)\\n                stack.append(child)\\n        return result\\n    \\n    # Initial retained: all node ids\\n    retained = set(node_by_id.keys())\\n    removed_order = []\\n    \\n    # Candidates: removable nodes that are not root and not required symbol\\n    # Sort by depth descending, then id ascending\\n    candidate_nodes = sorted(\\n        [n for n in nodes if n['removable'] and n['id'] != root_id and n['symbol'] not in required_symbols],\\n        key=lambda x: (-x['depth'], x['id'])\\n    )\\n    \\n    for cnode in candidate_nodes:\\n        cid = cnode['id']\\n        if cid not in retained:\\n            continue  # already removed\\n        # Get subtree ids\\n        subtree = subtree_ids(cid)\\n        # Check that no descendant is retained (i.e., all descendants must be already removed or candidate itself)\\n        # Actually we only want to remove if the entire subtree is not retained except maybe some already removed.\\n        # Since we are processing deepest first, the only retained descendants would be those not yet removed.\\n        # If any descendant is retained, we cannot remove this node.\\n        descendants_retained = [d for d in subtree if d != cid and d in retained]\\n        if descendants_retained:\\n            continue  # can't remove because would delete retained descendants\\n        # Check required symbols after removal\\n        # Compute remaining symbols\\n        remaining_symbols = set()\\n        for nid in retained:\\n            if nid not in subtree:  # not part of submerged subtree\\n                remaining_symbols.add(node_by_id[nid]['symbol'])\\n        # Check if all required symbols are still present\\n        if not required_symbols.issubset(remaining_symbols):\\n            continue\\n        # All conditions satisfied: remove this subtree\\n        removed_order.append(cid)\\n        for nid in subtree:\\n            retained.discard(nid)\\n    \\n    # Final retained list sorted\\n    retained_node_ids = sorted(retained)\\n    return {\\n        'removed_node_ids': removed_order,\\n        'retained_node_ids': retained_node_ids\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement `solve(case)` based on the provided task description. The task is to do grammar-guided node reduction on a parse tree. The payload contains nodes (each with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility) and required_symbols (list of symbols that must remain in the tree after reduction). The goal: produce a reduced tree by removing removable nodes while preserving required symbols and the root (which is not removable). The output should list removed_node_ids (in order of removal) and retained_node_ids (sorted). The algorithm: consider deeper nodes first (higher depth), then tie-break by node ID. Tentatively remove a node only if it's removable, not required symbol (symbol not in required_symbols? Actually required_symbols is a list of symbols; a node with that symbol is required? The description says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So nodes whose symbol is in required_symbols are required, cannot remove. Also nodes with children that are retained (i.e., after removal of some nodes, if a node's child is still present) cannot be removed because that would orphan children? The description: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So we must check that after removal, the tree still has at least one node for each required symbol.\n\nInterpretation: We have a tree. We can remove removable nodes if they are not required, not root, and no retained children (i.e., all its descendants will be removed as well? Actually if we remove a node, we also remove all its descendants because the tree structure is hierarchical. So removing a node removes its entire subtree. So we must ensure that after removal, required symbols are still present elsewhere in the tree. Also we cannot remove a node that has retained children? But if we remove the parent, the children are also removed, so the children are not retained. So condition \"node with retained children\" probably means we cannot remove a node if it still has children that are going to be retained (i.e., we cannot remove a node and keep its children because that would break the tree structure). In this tree, each node has a parent; if we remove a node, we must also remove all its descendants because they become disconnected. So the algorithm should consider removing a node only if it has no children that are going to be kept? Actually we are building the retained set; we decide which nodes to keep. So we cannot remove a node and keep any of its descendants. So when considering removal, we must ensure that all descendants are also being removed, and that the required symbols are not lost from the entire tree.\n\nThe given examples have nodes with depth. The root depth 0, children depth 1, grandchildren depth 2. The required_symbols are ['root','s1']. So nodes with symbol 's1' are required (cannot remove). Also root is required.\n\nWe need to implement an algorithm: \n1. Build a tree structure from the nodes list. Create a dict from id to node object.\n2. For each node, keep track of children.\n3. Identify removable nodes (removable=True), not root, not required symbol.\n4. Order nodes by depth descending, then by id (lexicographically? \"stable tie break at equal depth\" using node ID. Likely alphabetical order for IDs. The sample IDs are like 'n1','n2',... so alphabetical works. For deep-first, we need to process deeper nodes first.\n5. For each candidate node in that order, check if it can be removed:\n   - Must not be root, must have removable=True.\n   - Its symbol must not be in required_symbols.\n   - After removal (i.e., removing this node and all its descendants), check that the remaining nodes still contain at least one node for each required symbol. This can be done by simulating removal: temporarily remove that subtree and check if any required symbol becomes missing.\n   - Also must not have children that are retained? But if we are removing, we are also removing all descendants, so no children are retained. The condition \"node with retained children\" likely means if we intend to keep some descendant, we cannot remove the parent. But in our order, we are processing deepest first, so when we consider a node, its descendants may have already been removed or retained. We should not remove a node if any of its descendants are retained (i.e., not removed). But we are processing deepest first, so when we consider a node, we have already decided on its descendants. So we can check if any child (or descendant) is in the retained set. If yes, then we cannot remove this node because it would remove those retained nodes.\n6. If removal is allowed, add node's id to removed_node_ids list (in order of removal), and also mark all its descendants as removed (i.e., not retained). Remove them from retained set if they were previously considered? Actually we are building retained set by starting with all nodes? Or we start with all nodes as retained, then we remove. So initial retained set is all node ids. Then for each candidate we remove if conditions satisfied; then we remove that node and all its descendants from retained set. The order of removal: we record the node id being removed (the root of the removed subtree), not necessarily all descendants? The output asks for removed_node_ids array - likely the list of nodes that were selected for removal (the ones we explicitly removed). And retained_node_ids is the set of all nodes that are kept (sorted). So we should record when we decide to remove a node (the parent of the subtree), and afterwards that node and all its subtree are no longer in retained.\n\nThe algorithm:\n- retained = set(all node ids)\n- removed_order = []\n- candidate_nodes = sorted by (-depth, id) (descending depth, then ascending id)\n- for each n in candidate_nodes:\n   - if n.id not in retained: continue (already removed)\n   - if not n.removable: continue\n   - if n.id == root: continue\n   - if n.symbol in required_symbols: continue (required symbol)\n   - Check if any descendant of n is in retained? Actually if any descendant is retained (i.e., not removed yet), then we cannot remove n because that would remove those descendants. But note: if we process deepest first, we may have already removed some descendants. But if a descendant has been removed, then it's not in retained. So we need to check if there exists any descendant (direct or indirect) that is still in retained. That would mean removing n would remove those retained descendants, which is not allowed (since we only want to remove if the entire subtree is being removed). So condition: all descendants must not be in retained (i.e., already removed). However, if a descendant has been retained (maybe because it's required or not removable), then n cannot be removed. So we need to traverse the subtree and check if any descendant node id is in retained.\n   - Also check required symbols: after removing n and its subtree, the remaining retained nodes must cover all required symbols. Compute the set of retained symbols from retained set (excluding subtree). Then check if each required_symbol is present in that set.\n- If all conditions satisfied, then:\n   - removed_order.append(n.id)\n   - Remove n.id and all its descendants from retained. (We need to collect all descendant ids: we can do a DFS from n, gathering all node ids in its subtree, and then remove them from retained set.)\n\nAt the end, retained_node_ids = sorted list of remaining retained id strings.\nReturn {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_order, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_node_ids}.\n\nEdge cases: If a node is removed, its parent is now missing a child; but that's fine. The tree is reduced.\n\nWe also need to handle root: root's removable is False, so it won't be removed.\n\nThe required symbols check must be done after removing subtree: we need to see if any required symbol is present in retained set. Since retained set includes nodes not in subtree, we can compute symbol_to_node_ids mapping, or simply iterate over retained nodes and collect their symbols, then check if each required_symbol is present.\n\nAlso, we need to build parent-child relationships from the nodes list. We can create a dict node_by_id, then for each node, if parent is not None, add node to parent's children list (or store a child lookup). For subtree traversal, we need to find all descendants of a given node. We can either build an adjacency list (children) and do DFS from that node, or recursively use the parent links? Since we have parent id, we could build reverse map of parent->children. Easiest: create dictionary children_of = defaultdict(list). For each node, if parent is not None, children_of[parent].append(node.id). Then to get subtree, we can do BFS/DFS starting from node.id.\n\nImportant: The payload may have nodes that are not in a proper tree? But we assume it's valid. Also depth is given but we can ignore; we can compute depth or just rely on given depth for sorting.\n\nImplement solve(case) as described. Use only standard library.\n\nNow produce the final answer: a JSON with key \"implementation\" mapping to a string containing Python source code. Must escape properly.\n\nLet's write the code."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2169,"completion_tokens":2722,"total_tokens":4891,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":1949},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":121},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}